Dear all,
I am a regular in this group now and I have often found solutions to my problems here. It helps a lot and thanks to all.
I have a problem with a spatial recurrent event model that fails despite my efforts whose formula is:
formula2 <- inla.surv(times14, Status, subject) ~ Trait + Sex + f(Age, model="rw2",scale.model=TRUE) + f(Order, model="ar1") + f(spatial, model=CoxSPDE)

I hope that the nonlinearity of the variable "Age" is well written f(Age, model="rw2", scale.model = TRUE)

In fact, the events (successive infection) at the same individual are correlated between them but I am not sure to have written well the correlation with f(Order, model="ar1"). The variable "Order" is the order of the event in an individual identified by RID.  I wonder if I should write f(RID, model="ar1") or f(RID, control.group = list(model="ar1"))

What would you advise me to do with these formula4 to take into account the nonlinearity of the variable "Age" and the correlation between the infections of the same individual ?

PS: Attached is the output of the model I used

Bests regards

From udab85ab569953d94 at groups.google.com  Fri Mar 12 21:11:38 2021
From: udab85ab569953d94 at groups.google.com (Cici Chen)
Date: Fri, 12 Mar 2021 21:11:38 +0000
Subject: combination of predicted values in spatial-temporal model
Message-ID: <zghZJviTqBM.d0R_VYYRAwAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/zghZJviTqBM/m/d0R_VYYRAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear group,

I am hoping to get some help on how to get the combination of the predicted values in spatial-temporal models. Below I created a reproducible example using data from Chapter 8.6 of Bayesian inference with INLA by Virgilio Gómez-Rubio.

I used the brainNM dataset which provides observed and expected counts of cases of brain cancer in the counties of New Mexico from 1973 to 1991. Now suppose that I would like to predict the county-level new cases for 1992 and I am able to do so using the code below. However, my question is that,  how do I get the predicted total cases for 1992 with associated posterior summaries (i.e., 0.025 and 0.975 qunatiles)? Conceptually this is pretty straightforward, as it will be summing over all county-level prediction for 1992. But how do I code it up?

Thank you so much for your help!

###################
library("DClusterm")
data(brainNM)

nm.adj <- poly2nb(brainst@sp)
adj.mat <- as(nb2mat(nm.adj, style = "B"), "Matrix")

dat <- brainst@data
dat$id.time <- brainst@data$Year - 1973 + 1
dat$id.space <http://id.space> <- as.numeric(dat$ID)
dat$id.spacetime <- 1:nrow(dat)

## Fitting a model
prec.prior <- list(prec = list(param = c(0.001, 0.001)))

brain.st <http://brain.st> <- inla(Observed ~ 1 + f(Year, model = "rw1", hyper = prec.prior) +
        f(id.space <http://id.space>, model = "besag", graph = adj.mat, hyper = prec.prior) +
                 f(id.spacetime, model="iid", hyper=prec.prior),
                 data = dat, E = Expected, family = "poisson",
                 control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE, link = 1))

summary(brain.st <http://brain.st>)

### prediction
dat.tmp <- dat[dat$Year==1991, ]
dat.tmp$Observed  <- NA
dat.tmp$Expected <- 1

dat.pred <- rbind(dat, dat.tmp)
brain.st.pred <- inla(Observed ~ 1 + f(Year, model = "rw1", hyper = prec.prior) +
                   f(id.space <http://id.space>, model = "besag", graph = adj.mat, hyper = prec.prior) +
                   f(id.spacetime, model="iid", hyper=prec.prior),
                 data = dat.pred, E = Expected, family = "poisson",
                 control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE, link = 1))
summary(brain.st.pred)
### extract prediction of individual county
dat.pred$pred <- brain.st.pred$summary.fitted.values[, "mean"]
## how do I get the sum of the county of predicted values?

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Fri Mar 12 21:23:30 2021
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
