MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,

This depends a bit on whether you need total-count prediction, or if it’s enough to get sum-of-expectations prediction. In both cases, the general method is to use inla.posterior.sample(), from which you can compute/estimate the posterior distribution of any functional of the model. For count-prediction, you also need to add the data-level model simulation on top of the latent model simulation.

Some of this is made a bit easier in the inlabru package (github.com/inlabru-org/inlabru <http://github.com/inlabru-org/inlabru> ). See the Articles on the github.io <http://github.io> site linked from the README for examples from ecology. I think there’s an animal abundance estimation example there that even does something slightly more clever in the data-level step, by integrating the pdf:s instead of generating raw count samples.

Finn

